
=head1 USE IN SHELL-SCRIPTS

=head2 Advantages

The env utility is often used as the interpreter on the first line of interpreted 
scripts, as described in execve(2).

There are several advantages to doing so:

* The path to the interpreter does not need to be hard-coded. Not all systems
have perl in /usr/bin/perl for example. With env2, as long as perl is in the
PATH, it will be found and executed.

* Normally the interpreter needs to be an executable, not another script. With
env2, this restriction is removed. (Although Linux does not have this restriction.)

* Without env2, the interpreter arguments are treated differently on each platform. This
will cause problems when there are multiple arguments that are separated by
spaces that need to be passed in as separate arguments.  See the next sub-section
titled B<Splitting out the interpreter arguments string>.

=head2 Splitting out the interpreter arguments string

Each UN*X platform handles the interpreter arguments in their own unique (and broken) way.

On B<Linux>, the entire string following the interpreter name is passed as a single argument
to  the interpreter, and this string can include white space.

On B<Solaris>, the first white space terminates the args. Subsequent args
are silently discarded.

On B<macOS> an interpreter script can have multiple arguments, and white spaces
are used to delimit the arguments. This is a blind split and does not account for
single or double quoted or backslash escaped whitespace.

On B<Cygwin> the behavior is the same as on Linux: the whole string is passed as a
single argument (this is what B<configure> detects on Cygwin).

See the B<A BIT OF HISTORY> section for more.

=head2 Important note about the examples below

Because UN*X platforms may pre-split the args before the interpreter sees them,
it can be difficult for the script argument to be properly determined. This is especially
true if there are flags which do not start with a dash or if a flag takes an argument
that is separated from the flag by a space.  Because of this, env2 has a special
way to avoid this confusion. Use the -a (Allow) flag of env2 along with
the insertion of the B<~> character where needed.  For example, change:

    #!/usr/bin/env2 inter no_dash_arg -c c_arg

To

    #!/usr/bin/env2 -a inter ~no_dash_arg -c~c_arg

And the B<~> character will prevent the non-dashed argument from becoming the script
and shield the split on whitespace.  The B<~> is removed in the first instance
and replaced with a space in the second.

Also note that flags to env2 must all be grouped together:

  env2 -acdefnpsx
  
The following will cause problems on some platforms:

  env2 -a -c -e

=head2 Linux

The way the B<Linux> kernel parses the `#!' (first line) of an interpreted 
script is to treat all the interpreter args as a single string. So, if a
script named /usr/bin/script had a first line of:

    #!/usr/bin/php -n -q -d error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n -q -d error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"'
    arg[2] = '/usr/bin/script'

which will not work as php will not be able to parse arg[1].
However, when the first line is changed to use this program first:

    #!/usr/bin/env2 -a php -n -q -d~error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n'
    arg[2] = '-q'
    arg[3] = '-d'
    arg[4] = 'error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"'
    arg[5] = '/usr/bin/script'

(plus any arguments the user specified when executing script.) This will work
as expected.

=head2 Solaris

The way the B<Solaris> kernel parses the `#!' (first line) of an interpreted 
script is to silently discard all the interpreter args after the first one. So, if a
script named /usr/bin/script had a first line of:

    #!/usr/bin/php -n -q -d error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n'
    arg[2] = '/usr/bin/script'

which will NOT give the desired result.
However, when the first line is changed to use this program first:

    #!/usr/bin/env2 -a php -n -q -d~error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n'
    arg[2] = '-q'
    arg[3] = '-d'
    arg[4] = 'error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"'
    arg[5] = '/usr/bin/script'

(plus any arguments the user specified when executing script.) This will work
as expected.

=head2 macOS

The way the B<macOS> kernel parses the `#!' (first line) of an interpreted 
script is to blindly split the interpreter args on whitespace. So, if a
script named /usr/bin/script had a first line of:

    #!/usr/bin/php -n -q -d error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n'
    arg[2] = '-q'
    arg[3] = '-d'
    arg[4] = 'error_prepend_string="<span'
    arg[5] = 'style='color:'
    arg[6] = '#ff0000'>"'
    arg[7] = '/usr/bin/script'

which will NOT give the desired result.
However, when the first line is changed to use this program first:

    #!/usr/bin/env2 -a php -n -q -d~error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"

then the /usr/bin/php program will be started with the arguments of:

    arg[0] = '/usr/bin/php'
    arg[1] = '-n'
    arg[2] = '-q'
    arg[3] = '-d'
    arg[4] = 'error_prepend_string="<span style='color: #ff0000'>"'
    arg[5] = '/usr/bin/script'

(plus any arguments the user specified when executing script.) This will work
as expected.

=head1 QUOTING AND ESCAPES

B<env2> splits the interpreter argument string itself. By default it does this
like Perl's C<shellwords()>. Its original splitter, now called the legacy
splitter, is still available with the B<L> flag (see B<COMPATIBILITY>).

=head2 Shellwords splitting (the default)

By default, B<env2> splits exactly as Perl's C<shellwords()> does (the B<S> flag
also selects it, and is accepted for compatibility):

* Space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed and vertical tab separate
arguments. Runs of them count as one separator.

* Single quotes and double quotes group text, including whitespace, into one
argument, and are removed. Quoted and unquoted text that touch join into one
argument: B<ab"c d"'e'> is the single argument B<abc de>.

* Outside quotes and inside double quotes, a backslash makes the next character
literal and is removed: B<a\ b> is the single argument B<a b>.

* Inside single quotes everything is literal, backslashes included, except that
a backslash still stops a quote from ending the string: B<'a\'b'> is B<a\'b>.

* An empty pair of quotes is an empty argument.

* A quote that is never closed, or a backslash at the very end, is an error
(C<unmatched quote>).

This was checked against Perl itself on every string of up to six characters
built from the letters a and b, space, tab, single quote, double quote and
backslash (about 137,000 strings), and on the other whitespace characters;
there are no differences. B<make test> repeats the check. See
B<DEVELOPER_NOTES.txt> in the source.

The B<c> flag works with shellwords splitting (a B<#> that starts an argument,
and everything after it, is ignored). The B<s>, B<p> and B<x> flags only mean
something to the legacy splitter (they select it, see below), because the rules
above already say what happens to backslashes and empty arguments. The B<a> flag
(the B<~> hint) works as usual.

=head2 The legacy splitter (the L flag)

With B<L> (or with any of B<s>, B<p>, B<x>, unless B<S> is given too), B<env2>
uses the splitter it had before version 2.0. It follows these rules:

* Space, tab, newline and carriage return separate arguments. Runs of them
count as one separator.

* Single quotes and double quotes group text, including whitespace, into one
argument, and are removed. A quote of one kind can contain the other kind.

* A backslash is kept in the argument, unless the B<s> flag is used (see
below). The B<x> flag turns backslash sequences such as B<\n> and B<\t> into the
characters they stand for.

* With the B<s> flag, a backslash is removed when it comes before an ordinary
character or another backslash, inside single quotes as well as outside them.
A backslash before whitespace or a quote is left in place (see B<BUGS>).

* Empty arguments (C<""> or C<''>) are dropped, unless the B<p> flag is used.

* A quote that is never closed is an error (C<unmatched quote>), with one
exception (see B<BUGS>).

With B<s> and B<p>, these rules follow Perl's C<shellwords()> closely, and for
most input give the same arguments. (Without B<s> every backslash is kept, so
strings containing backslashes differ from C<shellwords()> wholesale.) The
remaining differences, found by the same comparison as above, are the reason
shellwords splitting became the default. They are:

* B<env2> keeps the backslash in front of whitespace or a quote even with B<s>,
where C<shellwords()> removes it and treats the character literally.

* With B<s>, B<env2> also removes backslashes inside single quotes, where
C<shellwords()> (like the shell) keeps single-quoted text exactly as written.

* Empty quotes: B<env2> drops them (without B<p>), where C<shellwords()> keeps
an empty argument. With B<p>, an empty pair of quotes directly followed by text
(two double quotes then B<abc>, say) gives an extra empty argument, where
C<shellwords()> gives just B<abc>.

* A single quote or double quote with nothing after it (a lone double quote,
for example) is silently ignored, where C<shellwords()> reports an error.

=head1 ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES IN THE #! LINE

With the B<E> flag, B<env2> replaces B<${NAME}> with the value of the
environment variable NAME, much as GNU env(1) does with its B<-S> option:

    #!/usr/bin/env2 -E ${PYTHON} -u

The rules:

* Only the braced form B<${NAME}> is recognised, where NAME is letters, digits
and underscores and does not start with a digit. Any other B<$> (B<$NAME>, B<$>
at the end, B<${}>, B<${NAME> with no closing brace) is an error. Write B<\$> for
a literal dollar sign.

* Expansion happens outside quotes and inside double quotes. Inside single
quotes B<${NAME}> is left as it is.

* The value is put into the argument as plain text. It is not split again, so a
value with spaces is still a single argument, and it is not expanded again.

* An unset variable is the empty string. An unquoted argument that is nothing
but an unset variable disappears; inside double quotes it is an empty argument;
B<pre${NAME}post> is B<prepost>. A variable that is set but has an empty value
gives an empty argument, as it does in GNU env(1).

* The variables set by the configuration file (see B<CONFIG FILE>) can be used:
with B<E> the configuration file is read before the C<#!> line is split.
Interpreter-argument lines in the configuration file are expanded too. The script
name and the arguments the user gives are never expanded.

* B<E> needs the shellwords splitter. Using it with B<L>, B<s>, B<p> or B<x>, which
select the legacy splitter, is an error.

Because the value comes from the environment of whoever runs the script,
expansion lets that person influence which interpreter runs and with what
arguments. That is the point of the feature, which is why it is off unless a
script asks for it with B<E>.

=head1 COMPATIBILITY

Before version 2.0 the legacy splitter was the only one. Starting with 2.0 the
default is shellwords splitting, so a C<#!> line (or an interpreter-argument line
in the config file) that contains quotes or backslashes can split differently.

* A line that uses B<s>, B<p> or B<x> is unchanged: those flags select the legacy
splitter, so it behaves exactly as before. (Give B<S> as well to get shellwords
splitting anyway; the flags then have no effect on splitting.)

* A line with none of them that has no quotes or backslashes is unchanged: both
splitters give the same arguments.

* Any other line may change. The main differences are that a backslash is now
removed (C<a\ b> is the one argument B<a b>), quoted pieces next to text join into
one argument, and empty quotes are kept as empty arguments.

* To get the old behaviour exactly, add B<L> to the flags.

=head1 CONFIG FILE

If a configuration file is found ($HOME/.env2rc) then it is parsed as follows:

    # These are environment variables           - this is a comment line
    VARIABLE1 = foo                             - set VARIABLE1
    VARIABLE2 = "with embedded spaces"          - set VARIABLE2 
    VARIABLE3 + additional                      - append to VARIABLE3
    VARIABLE4 !                                 - unset VARIABLE4
                                                - blank lines are ignored
    # These are program arguments               - another comment
    prog1 : -flag1                              - prepend -flag to prog1
    prog2 : -flag1 -arg2="val with spaces"      - prepend -flags to prog2
    prog3 ; -opt1                               - override flags with ones listed here

=head1 A BIT OF HISTORY

From 1993 to mid-2005, FreeBSD parsed out the tokens as
found on the first line of the script, and setup each token as a separate
value in arg[].  This extra processing did not match the behavior of other
OS's, and caused a few subtle problems.

In 2005, the BSD version of env(1) added the -S string option to split out the
interpreter arg line. This program DOES handle single and double quoted and backslash
escaped whitespace. It does NOT however reparse the arguments if 
they were pre-split by the kernel as is done on Solaris and macOS.

In 2018, the GNU version on env(1) added the same flag with slightly different
behaviour. This program DOES handle
single and double quoted whitespace. It does NOT handle backslash escaped whitespace
(a backslash followed by a space is an error; it offers C<\_> instead).
It also does NOT reparse the arguments if they were pre-split
by the kernel as is done on Solaris and macOS.

=head1 BUGS

A HOME path (or program name) longer than about 250 characters makes the config file be silently ignored.
In the legacy splitter (B<L>, or B<s>, B<p>, B<x>):
the B<s> flag does not remove a backslash that comes before whitespace or a quote;
with B<s>, backslashes inside single quotes are removed too (the shell keeps them);
with B<p>, an empty quote pair directly followed by text gives an extra empty argument;
and a quote with nothing after it is not reported as an unmatched quote.
The default shellwords splitter has none of these problems.
Code uses a strange mix of C and C++.
Please report all Bugs to the author.

